# Virtual Trading Simulator - Windows Setup Guide

## System Requirements
- Windows 10 or 11
- Node.js (version 14.0.0 or higher)
- MongoDB (local installation or MongoDB Atlas account)
- Git (optional, for version control)
- Visual Studio Code or any preferred code editor

## Installation Steps

### 1. Install Required Software

#### Node.js Installation
1. Download Node.js from [https://nodejs.org/](https://nodejs.org/) (LTS version recommended)
2. Run the installer and follow the installation wizard
3. Verify installation by opening Command Prompt and typing:
   ```
   node --version
   npm --version
   ```

#### MongoDB Installation (Choose one option)
**Option A: Local MongoDB Installation**
1. Download MongoDB Community Server from [https://www.mongodb.com/try/download/community](https://www.mongodb.com/try/download/community)
2. Run the installer and follow the installation wizard
   - Select "Complete" installation
   - Install MongoDB Compass (the GUI for MongoDB) when prompted
3. Create a data directory:
   ```
   mkdir C:\data\db
   ```
4. Start MongoDB service:
   ```
   "C:\Program Files\MongoDB\Server\{version}\bin\mongod.exe" --dbpath="C:\data\db"
   ```

**Option B: MongoDB Atlas (Cloud)**
1. Create an account at [https://www.mongodb.com/cloud/atlas](https://www.mongodb.com/cloud/atlas)
2. Create a new cluster (the free tier is sufficient)
3. Set up database access (create a user with password)
4. Set up network access (allow access from anywhere for development)
5. Get your connection string from the "Connect" button

### 2. Project Setup

#### Extract the Project
1. Extract the ZIP file containing the Virtual Trading Simulator project to a location of your choice (e.g., `C:\Projects\VirtualTradingSimulator`)

#### Configure Environment Variables
1. Navigate to the project's backend directory
2. Create a copy of the `.env` file if it doesn't exist
3. Update the following variables in the `.env` file:
   ```
   MONGO_URI=mongodb+srv://yourusername:yourpassword@yourcluster.mongodb.net/?retryWrites=true&w=majority
   LOCAL_MONGO_URI=mongodb://localhost:27017/virtualTrading
   JWT_SECRET=adisam1312VirtualTradingSimulator2024SecureKey
   PORT=5002
   NODE_ENV=development
   FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:3000
   YAHOO_FINANCE_API_KEY=X3NB3RQKYLP6RQTM
   NEWS_API_KEY=c05d8cf121aa46e5b46ed89152b80918
   ```
   - If using MongoDB Atlas, update the `MONGO_URI` with your connection string
   - If using local MongoDB, ensure `LOCAL_MONGO_URI` is correct

### 3. Install Dependencies

#### Install Backend Dependencies
1. Open Command Prompt as Administrator
2. Navigate to the project root directory:
   ```
   cd C:\path\to\VirtualTradingSimulator
   ```
3. Install dependencies:
   ```
   npm install
   ```

#### Install Frontend Dependencies
1. Navigate to the frontend directory:
   ```
   cd frontend
   ```
2. Install dependencies:
   ```
   npm install
   ```
3. Return to the project root:
   ```
   cd ..
   ```

### 4. Database Setup

#### Load Stock Data
1. From the project root directory, run:
   ```
   npm run load-stocks
   ```
   This will populate your database with Indian stock data.

#### Fix Database Indexes (if needed)
1. If you encounter any database index issues, run:
   ```
   npm run fix-indexes
   ```

### 5. Running the Application

#### Development Mode
1. Start the backend and frontend concurrently:
   - Open a Command Prompt window and navigate to the project root
   - Run the backend:
     ```
     npm run dev
     ```
   - Open another Command Prompt window, navigate to the frontend directory, and run:
     ```
     cd frontend
     npm start
     ```

2. Access the application:
   - Frontend: [http://localhost:3000](http://localhost:3000)
   - Backend API: [http://localhost:5002](http://localhost:5002)

#### Production Build
1. Create a production build:
   ```
   npm run build
   ```
2. Start the production server:
   ```
   npm run start:prod
   ```

### 6. Troubleshooting

#### MongoDB Connection Issues
- Ensure MongoDB service is running
- Check your connection string in the `.env` file
- Run the database connection fix script:
  ```
  npm run fix-db-connection
  ```

#### Node.js Memory Issues
- If you encounter memory issues, use the low-resource mode:
  ```
  npm run dev:lowres
  ```

#### Port Conflicts
- If port 5002 or 3000 is already in use, modify the `PORT` in `.env` file or the `proxy` in `frontend/package.json`

### 7. Additional Configuration

#### Real-time Data Setup
- Configure real-time market data updates:
  ```
  npm run setup-realtime-data
  ```

#### Monitor Database
- To monitor real-time updates to the database:
  ```
  npm run monitor-db
  ```

## Project Structure Overview

### Backend
- `/backend/config` - Configuration files
- `/backend/controllers` - API controllers
- `/backend/middleware` - Express middleware
- `/backend/models` - MongoDB models
- `/backend/routes` - API routes
- `/backend/services` - Business logic services
- `/backend/utils` - Utility functions

### Frontend
- `/frontend/src/components` - React components
- `/frontend/src/pages` - Page components
- `/frontend/src/redux` - Redux store configuration
- `/frontend/src/services` - API service calls
- `/frontend/src/utils` - Utility functions

## Features
- Virtual trading with Indian stocks
- Real-time market data
- Portfolio management
- Technical analysis
- Educational resources
- Virtual currency system (50 coins = 1 Rs)
- Free plan (5,000 coins) and Pro plan (25,000 coins)

## Notes
- This is a virtual trading simulator - no real money is involved
- The NEWS_API_KEY is already configured in the .env file
- For any issues, please refer to the troubleshooting section or contact support
